<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Finno-Soviet Treaty of 1948</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Agreement of Friendship, Cooperation and Mutual Assistance, YYA Treaty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Finno-Soviet Treaty of 1948, formally the Agreement of Friendship, Cooperation and Mutual Assistance, was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 6 April 1948. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> undertook to resist any attack by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> or its allies on itself, or on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> through Finnish territory, while the treaty acknowledged Finland's wish to stay outside great-power conflicts. Renewed several times, it framed Finnish foreign policy until it lapsed in 1992.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/finno-soviet-treaty-of-1948.jpg" alt="Finno-Soviet Treaty of 1948" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Government of Finland · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Finno-Soviet_Treaty_of_1948.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The treaty was known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> as the YYA Treaty, from the Finnish name Ystävyys-, yhteistyö- ja avunantosopimus, and in Swedish as the VSB-avtalet. It was concluded in the uncertain years after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continuation-war">Continuation War</a>, when relations between Finland and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> were still unsettled. The Soviets sought to deter Western or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allied Powers</a> from attacking the Soviet Union through Finnish territory, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> sought greater political independence from their neighbour, which had strategic regions such as the Kola Peninsula and the city of Leningrad close to the border.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> was bound to resist armed attacks by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> or its allies, in practice understood to mean the Western Bloc, against Finland or against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> through Finland, and if necessary to ask for Soviet military aid. The pact itself gave the Soviet military no right to enter Finland: any such step had to be agreed separately at Finland's request. Nor did it oblige Finland to act if the Soviet Union were attacked elsewhere. Its recognition of Finland's wish to stay out of great-power conflicts allowed a policy of neutrality in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Because of the treaty's wording and the state of relations, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-bloc">Eastern Bloc</a> and stayed out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-plan">Marshall Plan</a>, which prolonged its post-war hardship and made it more economically dependent on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. It kept its distance from Western military powers, from the proposed Scandinavian Defense Union and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>, and so fended off pressure to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a>. No joint military exercises with the Soviet Union were ever held.</p><p>The treaty was the main instrument of the Paasikivi-Kekkonen doctrine, the foreign policy of Paasikivi and of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urho-kekkonen">Urho Kekkonen</a> (1956-1981). It also gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> leverage in Finnish internal affairs, as in the note crisis, an influence called Finlandization; how far Kekkonen used it against his opponents is debated. There was also secret cooperation with the West, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-intelligence-agency">Central Intelligence Agency</a> funding of the Social Democratic Party to the sharing of seismic data on nuclear tests. The Soviet Union made similar agreements with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. In 1992 the treaty was replaced by a new one with post-Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Finno-Soviet Treaty of 1948” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
